From 4d3c765bf2dd9ab4c20cb6e3a9f4de8ab2516402 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 02:12:37 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Layouts of the data at the integration points array by array,
 automatic layout

The parameter QuadratureDataLayout accepts a layout for all the arrays of
a material (PointByPoint, ComponentByComponent or Automatic), or a set of
parameters giving the layout of each array (Gradients,
ThermodynamicForces, InternalStateVariables, ExternalStateVariables,
TangentOperator) and a padding (Padding). The environment variables
MFEM_MGIS_QUADRATURE_DATA_LAYOUT and MFEM_MGIS_QUADRATURE_DATA_PADDING
give the default values.

- Automatic, the default: component by component for a material whose
  behaviour is integrated by batches on a GPU, point by point otherwise.
  It is resolved as point by point for the generators of behaviour
  integrators which do not declare that they support the layouts (new
  argument of BehaviourIntegratorFactory::addGenerator).
- The padding adds unused values, set to NaN, to the leading dimension of
  the arrays: a code reading them without their views reads NaNs. It is a
  testing facility.
- The materials (MaterialArrays, Material, the behaviour integrators and
  the point-wise models) take a mgis::behaviour::MaterialDataManagerLayout.
- The accessors of the materials return partial quadrature functions
  built from the views of MGIS, in all the layouts.
- The external state variables with several components computed by an
  evaluator are given to MGIS in the layout of the material.
- The computations element by element (gradients, inner forces and
  stiffness matrices of an element, linearized operators, resultant force
  on a boundary) read and write the values of a point through the views
  (getPointValues, setPointValues): in place if the components of a point
  are contiguous, copied in a small buffer otherwise. Only the integration
  point by point requires the point by point layout, with or without
  padding.
- The thermodynamic forces in the global frame of an orthotropic material
  are stored in the layout of its thermodynamic forces.
