-several components, for instance by
-:code:`mgis::behaviour::setExternalStateVariable` with the values of all the
-points, must be stored in the layout of the material, as for :code:`MGIS`.
-The arrays of
-the material (members :code:`s0`, :code:`s1` and :code:`K` of the material
-data manager of :code:`MGIS`) are stored in its layout: a code reading them
-directly must use the views of :code:`MGIS`, which account for the layout
+are integrated on the host and others on a GPU (the :code:`Automatic`
+layout does it). There is no layout per device: the arrays of a material
+are computed where its behaviour is integrated, and their copies on the
+host and on the GPU share the same layout, so that the transfers copy the
+arrays as they are. The point-wise models (:code:`PointWiseModel`) accept
+the same parameter. The layout is fixed when the material is built, and does
+not change the results: the computations are the same, only the addresses
+of the values change.
+
+The value of the parameter may also be a set of parameters giving the
+layout of each array (:code:`Gradients`, :code:`ThermodynamicForces`,
+:code:`InternalStateVariables`, :code:`ExternalStateVariables` for the
+values of the external state variables with several components,
+:code:`TangentOperator`), the arrays which are not given keeping the
+layout given to the problem, and the padding of the arrays
+(:code:`Padding`, a non negative integer, zero by default):
+
+.. code-block:: cpp
+
+   problem.addBehaviourIntegrator(
+       ctx, "Mechanics", 1, library, behaviour,
+       {{"QuadratureDataLayout",
+         Parameters{{"TangentOperator", "ComponentByComponent"},
+                    {"Padding", 2}}}});
+
+The padding adds unused values to the leading dimension of the arrays
+(the stride between two points point by point, between two components
+component by component), set to NaN: any code reading the arrays without
+their views reads a NaN and fails loudly. It is a testing facility: the
+environment variable :code:`MFEM_MGIS_QUADRATURE_DATA_PADDING` pads the
+arrays of all the materials, for instance to run a whole test suite.
+
+The arrays of :code:`mfem-mgis` at the integration points follow the same
+rules: the work arrays of a material (deformation gradients and modified
+tangent operators of the F-bar formulation, thermodynamic forces in the
+global frame of an orthotropic material, copies on the GPU of its
+external state variables) are stored in the layout of the array they
+relate to, and the derivatives of the unknowns at the points of all the
+elements in the layout of the gradients given to the problem
+(:code:`Automatic`: :code:`ComponentByComponent` when the kernels compute
+on a GPU). The kernels of the finite elements (gradients, inner forces,
+tangent operator applied without assembly, Faltus correction) use one
+thread per point on a GPU, the contributions of the points to the nodes of
+an element being summed in their order: the results do not depend on the
+layouts, to the last bit. On the host, an element is processed by one
+thread.
+
+A material stored component by component requires the integration of its
+behaviour by batches: the integration point by point reads the values of a
+point as contiguous, and is refused at the setup of the problem (see
+below), as by the method :code:`integrate` of the behaviour integrators.
+The computations element by element (gradients, inner forces and
+stiffness matrices of an element, linearized operators given by
+:code:`getLinearizedOperators`, resultant force on a boundary given by
+:code:`computeResultantForceOnBoundary`) are made in all the layouts: the
+values of a point whose components are not contiguous are copied in a
+small buffer, the components of a point stored point by point being read
+in place.
+
+The accessors of the material (:code:`getGradient`,
+:code:`getThermodynamicForce`, :code:`getInternalStateVariable`...) return
+partial quadrature functions viewing its arrays with their strides, in
+both layouts. The call operator of a partial quadrature function returns
+the values of a point as a strided span (:code:`mgis::StridedSpan`),
+whatever the layout: the evaluators of :code:`MGIS` (:code:`as_stensor`,
+:code:`vmis`...) read them without copy, and give the same results to the
+bit in both layouts. The accessors returning the values of a point as
+contiguous values (:code:`getIntegrationPointValues`, :code:`data`) throw
+an exception if they are not. A partial quadrature function can be built
+with the component by component layout (constructor taking a
+:code:`mgis::FieldLayout`). An external state variable with several
+components computed by an evaluator is given to the material in the
+layout of the material: the result of the evaluator is used as is if it is
+stored densely in this layout, and copied in this layout otherwise. Values
+given directly to the material data manager of :code:`MGIS` for a variable
+with several components, for instance by
+:code:`mgis::behaviour::setExternalStateVariable` with the values of all
+the points, must be stored in the layout of the material, as for
+:code:`MGIS`. The arrays of the material (members :code:`s0`, :code:`s1`
+and :code:`K` of the material data manager of :code:`MGIS`) are stored in
+its layouts: a code reading them directly must use the views of
+:code:`MGIS`, which account for the layouts and for the padding
 (:code:`getGradientsView`, :code:`getThermodynamicForcesView`,
-:code:`getInternalStateVariableView`, :code:`getTangentOperatorBlocksView`...).
+:code:`getInternalStateVariableView`,
+:code:`getTangentOperatorBlocksView`...), or, for the values of a point,
+the functions :code:`mfem_mgis::getPointValues` and
+:code:`mfem_mgis::setPointValues`.
 
